I've written the fan review to `docs/content-analysis/pipeline-logs/2026-10-21_06-00-02-761_sombr/review-fan.json`. It passes the review schema, and its draft and evidence hashes match the frozen inputs by the runner's own hashing. I changed no other files and didn't open the other reviewer's output.

**Verdict:** the open case holds. He's 21 and has only been a star since spring 2025, and research opened 38 sources, so the record is thin because it's young, not because research was skipped. There's no blocker, and the title, description and FAQ all use "leading read" wording. There are 8 findings, 6 of them major:

1. **His background is missing.** The draft never mentions his dad's bands or learning guitar from him, his early-teen musical theatre, his years at LaGuardia as a voice student, or that "Caroline" went viral in 2022 and got him signed. "Caroline" shows up only as a bare name. It also says nobody has described him before 2022, while his own account of those years goes unused.
2. **The "industry plant" passage is unfair by omission.** It mentions only "his father's events company" and cuts his reply to "just incredibly insane." In the source, his point is that his dad's events work is AIDS fundraising.
3. **Quotes are trimmed to the half that supports the argument at that spot.** The CBS quote drops "I just never felt like I belonged," then calls what's left "the Three pattern." His line about being most himself on stage, used against Type 4, drops the next sentence: "I just feel out of place in the regular world." The "12 to 12" quote drops "I was so so passionate about that song."
4. **The "he turned modest after success" argument misreads him.** His humble lines in NYLON and on Broski's show are about being told as a kid it was "one in a million," which is what his parents told him. Only the Fallon "1,000-seater" line really contradicts his 2023 Madison Square Garden prediction.
5. **A well-known contradiction was cut.** Variety counted him calling himself "cocky" 12 times, and he told American Songwriter, "I want to impress Tony. I don't want him to have notes." The draft shows Berg only as someone he argues with.
6. **The Owen Thiele quote loses its setup.** In the same answer he said he "felt all this pressure to keep up in the pop world… it worked great." That's the closest the record comes to the choice between what feels true and what works, which the draft treats as still in the future.

The two minor findings: the draft says he studied the algorithm "at 18," but he said he did it in his early teens. And two separate ELLE statements are printed as one continuous quote.

I also marked five passages to keep, including the Madison Square Garden opening with its Knicks-game ending and the ELLE writer watching him watch her listen.

- **Length:** the draft is already about 2,800 words against a 2,500 target for open cases. The review tells the editor to pay for any restored context with the cuts already listed in the evidence log.
- **What I didn't check:** I checked quotes against the saved page texts and YouTube captions from the research stage, not fresh copies of the pages or the audio. Wording at the cited timestamps still needs a listen.
